KNOWNBUG
main.cpp
--cpp20 --unwind 6 --no-unwinding-assertions
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ invoke via pack-expansion decltype trait counts 1: SUCCESS$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
KNOWNBUG: a class template member typedef computed as
decltype(declval<F>()(declval<A>()...)) -- a decltype whose call expression
contains a pack expansion -- yields the wrong type when the class template is
instantiated with a parameter pack and that member type is used as a function's
return type, corrupting the return value.

Isolation: the same decltype works inline in a function and as a trailing
return type; a trivial member typedef works; a class-template decltype WITHOUT
pack expansion works (even when the class is instantiated with a pack); and a
class-template decltype WITH pack expansion works when the class is instantiated
with an explicit (non-pack) argument.  Only the *combination* fails: a class
template instantiated with a parameter pack <F, A...> whose member decltype
*expands that pack* (declval<A>()...).  The inner pack expansion is not bound to
the substituted pack arguments during class-template instantiation.

This is the std::invoke_result / __result_of_other_impl path; together with the
already-fixed [temp.deduct.call]/3 forwarding-reference pack deduction
(cpp11_forwarding_ref_pack) it is what std::reference_wrapper::operator() and
std::__invoke rely on, and hence what std::ref'd predicates and std::erase_if
(cpp20_vector_erase_if) need.

Refined root cause (investigated with the debug build): the member type is in
fact computed *correctly* in isolation -- instantiating
mini::invoke_result<F, int>::type for a lambda returning `long long` yields an
8-byte type (sizeof confirms), and invoke_result IS instantiated.  The failure
is in *nested function-template instantiation*: when `invoke` (whose return
type is invoke_result<F, A...>::type) is called from inside another template
(here count_matches), the nested instantiation of `invoke` does not complete and
the *caller's body is silently discarded* (count_matches's instantiated body is
empty, so it returns a nondet value -- e.g. 16384 -- and the assertion fails).
A direct, non-nested call to `invoke` is constant-folded to the correct result,
which is why the bug only surfaces through a template caller (or a loop that
prevents folding).

So the gap is not (only) expression-level pack expansion in template_mapt::apply
-- it is that instantiating a function template with a pack-expansion-decltype
class-trait return type during another template's body elaboration fails and
the failure is swallowed, discarding the caller's body.  Reclassify CORE once
the nested instantiation completes so std::ref'd predicates and std::erase_if
work.
